  "summary": "**Daily Health-Tech Digest — August 14, 2026**\n\nAI governance and security emerge as pressing infrastructure concerns, with healthcare organizations establishing new oversight bodies like CHAI's Health AI Cybersecurity Work Group and expanding councils focused on governance beyond initial deployment. Separately, Commure's termination of customer referral payments tied to AI products signals growing scrutiny of how AI vendors incentivize adoption, while Medicare's new payment incentives for AI-based devices are drawing researcher warnings about potential overuse despite offering hospitals financial benefits.\n\nThe biotech and therapeutics sector shows significant consolidation activity, with PTC Therapeutics acquiring Sangamo's gene therapy assets through bankruptcy auction for $211 million, Jazz Pharmaceuticals expanding in epilepsy through an $820 million acquisition, and multiple smaller biotech IPOs raising substantial capital for pain management and cardiac treatments. Separately, the OpenAI Foundation's $100 million hepatitis C commitment and California's CalRx insulin program—which has distributed over 120,000 packs at $55 each—represent direct funding efforts toward disease cure and drug affordability.\n\nOn the operational front, healthcare organizations are confronting growing pressures: a major ransomware attack exposed 3.8 million patient records at a revenue cycle vendor, home health providers face enrollment moratoriums requiring strategy resets, and employers are shifting away from the Big Three PBMs while seeking greater transparency and affordability. Ambulatory care networks are also discovering that rapid expansion has outpaced operational workflow evolution, creating growing service gaps and operational friction across payer-provider relationships that stakeholders describe as increasingly unsustainable.",
